 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Diotima’s Rachel Scott has always come across as an intellectual designer. This season she sounded almost mystical. She chalked it up to the deep dive she did into the work of artist Wifredo Lam last season; it seems to have left her especially receptive to other realms. On a long weekend trip to Grenada not long after that show was over in February, she said, “I started crying as the plane was landing. I felt like I’d been there before.” She shook her head. “I’d never been there.”</p><p>In a postshow interview, Scott elaborated on being visited by women of the past: “The sea is important for everyone, but I felt this really strong connection. I kept thinking about the beauty of it, but it’s also a burial ground of our ancestors. It just holds so much, and so I kept thinking about all these women from these different times.”</p><p>These muses, if you can call them that, inspired a spring collection in which Scott charted new territory, pushing further into tailoring and her three-dimensional organza intarsia technique—sometimes in a single garment, as with the trio of elongated trenches with dense organza ruffles on the skirts. And other times not: The organza intarsia draped on the bias made its dress undulate like a wave. She also explored new ways to create surface interest, splicing chino twill, an “out-of-context” fabric for evening, into little strips and resewing them “so that it looks kind of like a lattice but also like sargassum.”</p><p>Most promising of the new developments were the clingy siren dresses with cascades of ruffles spilling down their fronts. The pair of them she put on the runway had an easy sexiness that will appeal to women looking for a special after-dark dress. Second-skin matte viscose knit tops, skirts, and tube dresses with horsehair ruffle hems also had obvious commercial prospects. What there could have been more of was the crochet that made Scott’s early work so distinctive and that you see so much of on guests at her shows; instead, she used it only as insets. As her brand grows and Scott challenges herself to try new things, she shouldn’t be too quick to let go of her own past.</p></div>",
